===Etymology===
From {{etyl|LL.}} {{term|actus reus||guilty act|lang=la}}, from {{etyl|la}} {{term|actus|āctus|act}} + {{term|reus||guilty}}, after {{term|mens rea}}.

===Pronunciation===
*{{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˌaktəsˈɹeɪəs/|/ˌaktəsˈɹiːəs/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|actus rei}}

# {{legal}} A [[criminal]] act, considered as separate from the intentions or state of mind of the [[perpetrator]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#*'''2011''', {{w|Steven Pinker}}, ''The Better Angels of Our Nature'', Penguin 2012, p. 610:
#*:A brute emotional reflex to the '''''actus reus''''', the bad act (‘She killed her husband! Shame!’), could trigger an urge for retribution regardless of her intention.

